course: dictionary
lesson: 9430
title: A place with rows of seats
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A stadium is a big place with rows of seats around a field or track, where people watch sports or concerts. You can work out its meaning from the words near it, like «the big football stadium», which tells you it is large and built for matches.
explain: «At the ancient Olympic Games, cheats paid fines that built statues, and everyone walking into the stadium remembered them.» The word ancient tells you the games were very old, and the stadium is the place where the games happened. Here the stadium is the setting, the place the story is about.
explain: Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence in place of the hard word? Then test your idea by reading the sentence again. If «a big place with seats» makes sense in «walking into the stadium», your meaning is right.
fact: A stadium is a big place with rows of seats around a field or track.
fact: People go to a stadium to watch football, rugby or baseball, or to see big concerts.
fact: The plural of stadium is «stadiums», and «stadia» is also a correct plural.
fact: A Roman «vomitorium» was a wide passage that let crowds pour out of a stadium, not a room for being sick.
fact: Stay safe at a stadium: stay with your adult, know where the exits are, and meet at a set place if you get lost.
exercise: «Noah told a player his house was near the big football stadium.» What is a stadium? | A kind of ball used in football | A small shop on the high street | A quiet room for reading | *A big place with rows of seats around a field or track
exercise: «At the ancient Olympic Games, cheats paid fines.» Find and copy the word that tells you the games were very old. | *ancient | cheats | fines | Olympic
exercise: If you get lost at a stadium, what should you do? | Hide under a seat until it is quiet | Wander up and down the rows looking alone | *Go to the set meeting place you agreed with your adult | Walk out of the exit by yourself to find a taxi
test: What does «stadia» mean? | *More than one stadium | A small seat near the front | A stadium that is very old | A stadium with a roof
test: «A Roman vomitorium was a wide passage that let crowds pour out of a stadium.» What did a vomitorium do? | Held the seats in place | Sold tickets to the games | *Let crowds pour out of the stadium | Made people feel sick
exam: «Small details, like a nearby stadium or shop, can add up to where you live.» What does this sentence suggest? | Only big buildings matter to a place | *Little things can add up to make a place feel like home | Stadiums are always far away from homes | Shops are more important than people
